\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=27mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\title{Reusing partial graphs in a classification of order forty}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We recover the unique edge-girth-regular graph of degree six,
girth five and order forty with twenty-two pentagons through each
edge. The previously proved reduction to five local configurations
leaves only edges between thirty leaves undecided. Isomorphic
partial graphs share their remaining classification. A finite
directed acyclic graph records every required branch and every
permutation used for reuse. Its separate checker reconstructs the
branches and verifies the permutations; it requires no graph
isomorphism software.
\end{abstract}

\section{The acquired mathematical reduction}
Our reviewed note \emph{Three exterior vertices in a graph of
girth five}, Lemmas~1--2, applies to
\[
       n=k^2+4,\qquad \lambda=(k-1)^2-3,\qquad k\geq4.
\]
It proves that the three vertices outside the Moore ball of any
root are independent and each meets one leaf in every branch.
No pair of exterior vertices shares a leaf in two branches.
There are therefore five forms for these incidences, up to
relabelling: $j=0,1,2,3$ distinct pair coincidences in separate
branches, or a triple coincidence in one branch. All remaining
branches have three separate neighbours.

For $k=6$ the ball contains a root, six neighbours and thirty
leaves, five in each branch. Together with the three exterior
vertices this gives forty vertices. The only undecided edges
join leaves. The root, its six neighbours and the exterior
vertices have their final degrees. We retain the four colours
``root'', ``root neighbour'', ``leaf'' and ``exterior vertex''
on every partial graph.

\begin{lemma}\label{transport}
Within any fixed one of the five forms, a colour-preserving
isomorphism between partial graphs induces a bijection between
their admissible completions.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
All prescribed edges have an endpoint outside the leaf colour,
and none of these incidences may change. All other edges join
leaves. A colour-preserving isomorphism consequently takes the
prescribed incidence structure to itself and transports exactly
the possible additional edges. It preserves degrees and cycle
lengths, including the number of pentagons through each edge.
Its inverse provides the inverse transport of completions.
\end{proof}

\section{A finite certificate of complete coverage}
Every node of the certificate contains a literal adjacency matrix
and the identifier of its initial form. A node is checked first
to contain exactly the prescribed edges outside the leaf set,
to have degrees at most six, and to contain no triangle or
quadrangle. Its successors have one of the following meanings.

At an \emph{expansion node}, choose a deficient leaf $v$ needing
$s$ edges. Its candidate neighbours are all deficient leaves
whose individual joining edge creates no triangle or quadrangle.
If any deficient leaf has fewer candidates than it needs, the
node fails and has no successors. Otherwise the successors
are precisely all choices of $s$ candidates that can be joined
to $v$ without creating a short cycle. The existing complete
extension argument proves that these choices exhaust all
completions of the partial graph.

The checker reconstructs this list independently. Besides checking
each candidate individually, two selected candidates must be
nonadjacent and have no common neighbour. These are exactly the
new triangles and quadrangles using two new edges at $v$.
A simple cycle cannot use more than two edges at $v$, so there
is no other new short cycle to test. The resulting literal
adjacency matrices must equal the complete recorded child list,
without omissions or duplicate children.

At a \emph{reuse node}, the record names an earlier node in the
same form, with the same number of edges, and supplies a vertex
permutation. The checker verifies that it is a bijection, preserves
the four colours and transports every adjacency entry exactly.
Lemma~\ref{transport} then identifies the remaining problems.
There are no additional children at a reuse node.

Finally, a full graph has no children. Its degrees, connectedness,
girth and individual pentagon counts are checked directly. The
qualifying terminal graph is identified with the stored source
example by another explicit permutation, checked on every
vertex pair.

\begin{lemma}\label{dag}
If all five initial nodes and all their records pass these checks,
the qualifying terminal graphs contain every isomorphism class
with the stated parameters.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are at most $120$ edges in a partial graph of degree at most
six on forty vertices. Give a node of index $i$ and edge count $e$
the ordered pair $(120-e,i)$. An expansion successor has strictly
more edges, so its first coordinate decreases. A reuse successor
has the same edge count and an earlier index, so its second
coordinate decreases. Every dependency thus decreases this pair
in lexicographic order. It follows that the finite dependency
graph is acyclic.

Induction in this order proves coverage. A failed node has no
completion because a required degree cannot be filled. A full
node is checked directly. At an expansion node every completion
contains exactly one of the listed neighbour sets of $v$, and
is covered by that child's induction hypothesis. At a reuse node,
Lemma~\ref{transport} reduces the claim to its earlier counterpart.
The five initial forms exhaust all possible rooted graphs by the
acquired reduction, proving the assertion.
\end{proof}

\section{The classification}
The checked certificate has the following sizes. The numbers count
partial graphs in the certificate, not isomorphism classes of
complete unrooted graphs.
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
\text{form}&\text{nodes}&\text{reuse nodes}&\text{full graphs}\\\hline
j=0&1222&912&1\\
j=1&239&226&0\\
j=2&567&383&0\\
j=3&2125&901&0\\
\text{one triple}&220&114&0
\end{array}
\]
There are $4373$ nodes and $2536$ verified reuse permutations.
The sole full graph is isomorphic, by the supplied bijection, to
\nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_305}. It has $528$ pentagons, with
twenty-two through each of its $120$ edges.

\begin{theorem}
The minimum order for $(k,g,\lambda)=(6,5,22)$ is forty,
and exactly one isomorphism class occurs at that order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Moore ball gives the lower bound $1+6+6\cdot5=37$.
Pentagon incidence gives $5\mid |E(G)|\lambda=66|V(G)|$,
so the order is divisible by five and is at least forty.
The certificate and Lemma~\ref{dag} give at most one class at
forty, while its directly checked terminal graph proves existence.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and verification.}
The source entry is Table~4, physical page~20, of J.~Goedgebeur
and J.~Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular
graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024. Its earlier lower
bound is attributed to Exoo and Jajcay's cage survey, reference~[10];
its earlier example is attributed to Kiss, Miklavi\v{c} and
Sz\H{o}nyi, reference~[15]. We retain these attributions and
claim no historical priority for the classification.

The common five-form reduction is reused without a new proof.
The contribution of this note is the explicit transport argument
and complete finite certificate for the degree-six application.
A canonical-labelling program helped propose repeated states;
its correctness is not a premise of the certificate. The checker
reads only the adjacency records, verifies every branch and
permutation, and checks the terminal graph directly. These are
finite computational certificates, not a Lean formalization.
\end{document}
